clueweb09-en0008-06-11060.0 Lipoma (Tumor) SearchQuery Comprehensive Diabetes Care at UAB Comprehensive Diabetes Care at UAB Ovalle_Video_Promo.jpg Dear Doctor Column, January 19, 2004 Lipoma Brain Tumor Benign, Slow-growing Question: I was diagnosed with a lipoma on my brain. I understand that this is a fatty tumor, but how serious is this condition and what is the treatment? Answer: Lipomas are non-cancerous, slow-growing tumors composed of fat tissue. Lipomas usually grow in the neck, shoulders, back, or arms; it's rarer to have a lipoma in the brain. When they do appear in the brain, the most common location is in the region of the corpus callosum (the structure that connects the two hemispheres of the brain), although they can occur anywhere in the brain. A lipoma in the brain may cause no symptoms and is often diagnosed coincidentally when magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) or computed tomography (CT) scans are performed for other medical reasons.
