clueweb09-en0009-61-32927.0 [] [] [] [] [] [] [] Tumors of the Adipose (Fat) Tissue in Cats and Dogs [html hit counter] Lipomas (benign tumors) Lipomas refer to benign (noncancerous) tumors of the fat tissue that can be distinguished from the malignant liposarcoma tumors by cytology or biopsy. They are relatively common in older dogs, especially in subcutaneous locations (the layer of tissue directly underlying the skin), however, they can also occur in the chest cavity, abdominal cavity, spinal canal, and the vulva nad vagina of dogs. They rarely cause any symptoms but can become a medical problem once they reach a size large enough that can compress onto and interfere with adjacent tissues/organs. Once the lipomas begin to cause medical problems in the pets, they are usually surgically removed which can be followed by radiation therapy to ensure that the tumor won't grow back. Intermuscular lipomas are a variant of the
